## 🔹 PART 1 — VIRTUAL ENVIRONMENT

### What is it and why do we need it?
A virtual environment is like a clean room just for your project. It keeps all the packages
(Django, etc.) you install isolated to this project only. Without it, packages installed
globally on your machine can conflict with each other across different projects.

### Step 1 — Create the environment
Run this in your terminal inside your project folder:

```bash
python -m venv env
```

> `env` is the name of the environment folder. It will appear in your project directory.
> You can name it anything, but `env` is the standard convention.

### Step 2 — Activate the environment

**Windows:**
```bash
env\Scripts\activate
```

**Mac / Linux:**
```bash
source env/bin/activate
```

**✅ How to know it worked:**
Your terminal prompt will now show `(env)` at the beginning:
```
(env) C:\your-path\your-project>
```

> ⚠️ **Important:** You must activate the environment every time you open a new terminal
> before working on your project. If you don't see `(env)`, it's not active.

