## 🔹 PART 2 — INSTALL DJANGO & CREATE PROJECT

### Step 3 — Install Django

```bash
pip install django==5.2
```

> **Why version 5.2 specifically?**
> Django releases two types of versions:
> - **LTS (Long-Term Support):** Supported and patched for several years. ✅ Use this.
> - **Non-LTS (Latest):** Has newer features but stops receiving updates quickly. ❌ Avoid for production.
>
> Django 5.2 is the current LTS version. It's stable, well-documented, and the safest choice.

### Step 4 — Create the Django project

```bash
django-admin startproject config .
```

> **What is `config`?** It's the name of your main project configuration folder.
> It will contain your `settings.py`, `urls.py`, and other project-level files.
>
> **What is the `.` (dot) at the end?** This is CRITICAL.
> The dot tells Django: *"Create the project files right here in the current folder."*
> Without the dot, Django creates an extra nested folder which makes the structure messy.
>
> ✅ With dot: `manage.py` is in the same folder as your project
> ❌ Without dot: `manage.py` gets buried one level deeper inside another folder

### Step 5 — Test the server (just to confirm Django is working)

```bash
python manage.py runserver
```

Open your browser and visit: **http://127.0.0.1:8000/**

You should see the Django rocket welcome page. If you do — Django is installed and working. ✅

> Press `Ctrl + C` in the terminal to stop the server.

