## 🔹 PART 3 — CREATE & REGISTER YOUR APP

### What is a Django "App"?
In Django, a **project** is the whole website. An **app** is a specific section of functionality
inside that project. For example, if you were building a school system:
- `students` could be one app (handles student data)
- `courses` could be another app (handles courses)
- `nameofapp` is the standard name for your main/general app

### Step 6 — Create the app

```bash
python manage.py startapp nameofapp
```

This creates a `nameofapp/` folder with the files Django needs (views.py, models.py, etc.).

### Step 7 — Register the app in settings

Open `config/settings.py` and find the `INSTALLED_APPS` list. Add your app to it:

```python
INSTALLED_APPS = [
    'django.contrib.admin',       # The built-in admin panel
    'django.contrib.auth',        # Built-in user authentication
    'django.contrib.contenttypes',
    'django.contrib.sessions',
    'django.contrib.messages',    # Built-in flash messages
    'django.contrib.staticfiles', # Handles CSS/JS/images
    'django.contrib.humanize',    # ← Add this too (for readable dates, numbers)

    # Your own apps go below:
    'nameofapp',
]
```

> ⚠️ If you skip registering an app, Django won't recognize it — your models,
> views, and URLs won't work. Always register every app you create.

