## 🔹 PART 4 — CONFIGURE SETTINGS (Templates & Static Files)

### Step 8 — Configure Templates

Django needs to know *where* to look for your HTML files. Open `config/settings.py` and
find the `TEMPLATES` section. Update the `DIRS` line like this:

```python
TEMPLATES = [
    {
        'BACKEND': 'django.template.backends.django.DjangoTemplates',
        'DIRS': [BASE_DIR / 'templates'],  # ← Tell Django to look in the 'templates' folder
        'APP_DIRS': True,
        'OPTIONS': {
            'context_processors': [
                'django.template.context_processors.request',
                'django.contrib.auth.context_processors.auth',
                'django.contrib.messages.context_processors.messages',
            ],
        },
    },
]
```

> `BASE_DIR / 'templates'` means: *"Look for a `templates` folder in the root of my project."*
> This is where all your `.html` files will live.

### Step 9 — Configure Static Files

Still in `config/settings.py`, scroll to the bottom and add/update these lines:

```python
STATIC_URL = '/static/'

STATICFILES_DIRS = [
    BASE_DIR / 'static',  # ← Tell Django where your CSS/JS/image files are
]

DEFAULT_AUTO_FIELD = 'django.db.models.BigAutoField'
```

> **What are static files?** Files that don't change dynamically — like your CSS stylesheet,
> JavaScript files, and images. Django needs to know where they live.

### Step 10 — Configure Messages (Flash Alerts)

Add this anywhere in `config/settings.py` to connect Django message types to Tailwind CSS classes:

```python
from django.contrib.messages import constants as messages

MESSAGE_TAGS = {
    messages.DEBUG:   'bg-gray-100 border border-gray-300 text-gray-700',
    messages.INFO:    'bg-blue-50 border border-blue-300 text-blue-700',
    messages.SUCCESS: 'bg-green-50 border border-green-400 text-green-700',
    messages.WARNING: 'bg-yellow-50 border border-yellow-400 text-yellow-700',
    messages.ERROR:   'bg-red-50 border border-red-400 text-red-700',
}
```

### Step 11 — Create the Required Folders

Run these in your terminal (in your project root):

```bash
mkdir templates
mkdir static
mkdir static\src
```

> **On Mac/Linux**, use forward slashes: `mkdir -p static/src`

Then create a file at `static/src/input.css`. Inside it, write:

```css
@import "tailwindcss";
```

> This one line is the entry point for Tailwind. It pulls in all of Tailwind's styles.
> Tailwind will compile this into a full `output.css` file automatically.

