## 🔹 PART 5 — INSTALL NODE.JS & TAILWIND CSS

### Why do we need Node.js for a Python project?
Tailwind CSS is a JavaScript-based tool. It needs Node.js to run its compiler,
which reads your HTML files and generates only the CSS you're actually using.
Django itself doesn't need Node — only Tailwind does.

### Step 12 — Install Node.js

Download and install the **LTS version** from the official site:
👉 **https://nodejs.org**

After installation, verify it worked:
```bash
node -v
npm -v
```

Both commands should print a version number. If they do, Node is installed. ✅

### Step 13 — Initialize a Node project

Run this in your project root folder:

```bash
npm init -y
```

> This creates a `package.json` file in your project. Think of it as Node's version
> of `requirements.txt` — it tracks all your JavaScript dependencies and scripts.
> The `-y` flag just says "yes" to all default questions automatically.

### Step 14 — Install Tailwind CSS

```bash
npm install tailwindcss @tailwindcss/cli --save-dev
```

> This installs:
> - `tailwindcss` — the nameofapp framework
> - `@tailwindcss/cli` — the command-line tool that compiles your CSS
> - `--save-dev` — saves them as development dependencies (not needed in production)

### Step 15 — Add the build script to `package.json`

Open your `package.json` file. Find the `"scripts"` section and update it like this:

```json
"scripts": {
  "dev": "npx @tailwindcss/cli -i ./static/src/input.css -o ./static/src/output.css --watch"
}
```

> **What does this script do?**
> - `-i ./static/src/input.css` → reads your input file
> - `-o ./static/src/output.css` → compiles and writes the final CSS to output.css
> - `--watch` → keeps running and automatically recompiles whenever you save an HTML file
>
> You run this with `npm run dev`.

### Step 16 — Run Tailwind (keep this terminal running)

```bash
npm run dev
```

> **Important:** This terminal must keep running the entire time you're working.
> Open a *second* terminal for Django. Do not close this one.
> When Tailwind is running, you'll see it watching for file changes.

