## 🔹 PART 6 — BASE TEMPLATE & HOME PAGE

### Step 17 — Create the base template

Every page on your site will "extend" this base template. It contains your
navbar, CSS link, and the message display — shared across all pages.

Create: `templates/base.html`

```html
{% load static %}

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>{% block title %}My Django App{% endblock %}</title>

    <!-- This links the compiled Tailwind CSS file -->
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{% static 'src/output.css' %}">
</head>

<body class="bg-gray-50 min-h-screen">

    <!-- NAVBAR -->
    <nav class="bg-green-600 text-white p-4 shadow-md">
        <div class="container mx-auto flex justify-between items-center">
            <h1 class="text-xl font-bold">My App</h1>
            <div class="space-x-4 text-sm">
                <a href="{% url 'home' %}" class="hover:text-green-200 transition">Home</a>
                <a href="{% url 'item_list' %}" class="hover:text-green-200 transition">Items</a>
                <a href="{% url 'item_create' %}" class="hover:text-green-200 transition">Add New</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </nav>

    <!-- DJANGO FLASH MESSAGES (success/error/warning alerts) -->
    {% if messages %}
    <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto mt-4 px-4">
        {% for message in messages %}
        <div class="{{ message.tags }} px-4 py-3 rounded-lg mb-3 shadow-sm font-medium">
            {{ message }}
        </div>
        {% endfor %}
    </div>
    {% endif %}

    <!-- PAGE CONTENT (each page fills this block) -->
    <div class="container mx-auto p-6">
        {% block content %}{% endblock %}
    </div>

</body>
</html>
```

> **Key concepts here:**
> - `{% load static %}` — must be at the top so Django can find your static files
> - `{% block title %}` — child pages can override what shows in the browser tab
> - `{% block content %}` — child pages inject their HTML here
> - `{% url 'home' %}` — generates the URL for a named route (we define these in urls.py)

