## 🔹 PART 7 — ROUTING (URLs)

### How Django routing works:
When someone visits a URL (like `/items/`), Django looks at your `urls.py` files to
figure out which view function to call. There are two levels:
1. **Project-level URLs** (`config/urls.py`) — the main entrance
2. **App-level URLs** (`nameofapp/urls.py`) — handles routes specific to the `nameofapp` app

### Step 19 — Create app-level URLs

Create a new file: `nameofapp/urls.py`

```python
from django.urls import path
from . import views

urlpatterns = [
    path('', views.home, name='home'),                               # Home page → /
    path('items/', views.item_list, name='item_list'),               # List all items → /items/
    path('items/new/', views.item_create, name='item_create'),       # Create form → /items/new/
    path('items/<int:pk>/', views.item_detail, name='item_detail'),  # View one item → /items/1/
    path('items/<int:pk>/edit/', views.item_update, name='item_update'),    # Edit → /items/1/edit/
    path('items/<int:pk>/delete/', views.item_delete, name='item_delete'),  # Delete → /items/1/delete/
]
```

> - `name='home'` → lets you use `{% url 'home' %}` in templates instead of hardcoding `/`
> - `<int:pk>` → a dynamic segment that captures the item's ID from the URL

### Step 20 — Connect app URLs to the project

Open `config/urls.py` and update it:

```python
from django.contrib import admin
from django.urls import path, include

urlpatterns = [
    path('admin/', admin.site.urls),      # Built-in admin panel
    path('', include('nameofapp.urls')),       # All nameofapp app URLs
]
```

> `include('nameofapp.urls')` — tells Django: *"pass any URL not matched above to nameofapp/urls.py"*

