## 🔹 PART 10 — FULL CRUD SYSTEM

CRUD = **C**reate, **R**ead (List + Detail), **U**pdate, **D**elete.
This is the foundation of almost every web application.

### Step 23 — Create the Model (`nameofapp/models.py`)

A model is a Python class that represents a database table. Each attribute is a column.

```python
from django.db import models

class Item(models.Model):
    title = models.CharField(max_length=200)          # Short text field
    description = models.TextField()                   # Long text field (no limit)
    created_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True)  # Auto-set when created
    updated_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now=True)      # Auto-updated on every save

    def __str__(self):
        return self.title  # Controls what Django shows in the admin panel
```

After creating your model, run migrations again:

```bash
python manage.py makemigrations
python manage.py migrate
```

### Step 24 — Register the Model in Admin (`nameofapp/admin.py`)

This makes your model visible and manageable in the Django admin panel.

```python
from django.contrib import admin
from .models import Item

admin.site.register(Item)
```

### Step 25 — Create the Form (`nameofapp/forms.py`)

Django forms let you create HTML forms tied directly to your model.
Create a new file: `nameofapp/forms.py`

```python
from django import forms
from .models import Item

class ItemForm(forms.ModelForm):
    class Meta:
        model = Item              # Which model this form is based on
        fields = ['title', 'description']   # Which fields to show
        widgets = {
            'title': forms.TextInput(attrs={
                'class': 'w-full px-4 py-3 border border-gray-300 rounded-xl focus:outline-none focus:border-green-500 transition',
                'placeholder': 'Enter title here...'
            }),
            'description': forms.Textarea(attrs={
                'class': 'w-full px-4 py-3 border border-gray-300 rounded-xl focus:outline-none focus:border-green-500 transition h-36',
                'placeholder': 'Enter description here...'
            }),
        }
```

> **What are widgets?** They control how each field is rendered in HTML.
> The `attrs` dictionary adds HTML attributes like `class` (for Tailwind styling) and `placeholder`.

### Step 26 — Create All CRUD Views (`nameofapp/views.py`)

Replace the content of `nameofapp/views.py` with this complete set of views:

```python
from django.shortcuts import render, get_object_or_404, redirect
from django.contrib import messages
from .models import Item
from .forms import ItemForm


# ─── HOME ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def home(request):
    return render(request, 'home.html')


# ─── LIST (Read all) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def item_list(request):
    items = Item.objects.all().order_by('-created_at')  # Newest first
    return render(request, 'nameofapp/item_list.html', {'items': items})
    # The dict {'items': items} passes data to the template


# ─── DETAIL (Read one) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def item_detail(request, pk):
    item = get_object_or_404(Item, pk=pk)  # Gets item or shows 404 if not found
    return render(request, 'nameofapp/item_detail.html', {'item': item})


# ─── CREATE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def item_create(request):
    if request.method == 'POST':
        # Form was submitted — process it
        form = ItemForm(request.POST)
        if form.is_valid():
            form.save()
            messages.success(request, '✅ Item created successfully!')
            return redirect('item_list')
    else:
        # Page was just opened — show an empty form
        form = ItemForm()
    return render(request, 'nameofapp/item_form.html', {'form': form, 'action': 'Create'})


# ─── UPDATE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def item_update(request, pk):
    item = get_object_or_404(Item, pk=pk)
    if request.method == 'POST':
        form = ItemForm(request.POST, instance=item)  # instance= ties form to existing record
        if form.is_valid():
            form.save()
            messages.success(request, '✏️ Item updated successfully!')
            return redirect('item_detail', pk=item.pk)
    else:
        form = ItemForm(instance=item)  # Pre-fills form with existing data
    return render(request, 'nameofapp/item_form.html', {'form': form, 'action': 'Update', 'item': item})


# ─── DELETE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def item_delete(request, pk):
    item = get_object_or_404(Item, pk=pk)
    if request.method == 'POST':
        item.delete()
        messages.error(request, '🗑️ Item deleted.')
        return redirect('item_list')
    return render(request, 'nameofapp/item_confirm_delete.html', {'item': item})
```

> **Pattern to notice:** Every view that handles a form uses the same two-path logic:
> - `POST` → form was submitted, process it
> - `GET` (everything else) → page just loaded, show the empty or pre-filled form

